\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Input the foundation model receives, and output it returns, for three representative Wilson configurations chosen to span the relevant behaviour: a four-fermion configuration driving an energy-growing tail (red, $c_{\ell q}^{(3)} = +0.55$ with a mild $c_{\ell q}^{(1)}$ cancellation), a moderate energetic configuration (blue, $c_{\ell q}^{(1)} = +0.30$ with sub-leading $c_{Hq}^{(1,3)}$), and a vertex-dominated configuration that barely departs from the Standard Model (grey, $c_{Hq}^{(3)} = +0.40$). \textbf  {Left:} the foundation model's view at inference. For each configuration we draw $K = 12$ context points $(m_{\ell \ell }, \mu )$ from the analytic oracle at $m_{\ell \ell }$ values sampled uniformly on $[0.30, 2.30]$~TeV; the underlying $\mu (m_{\ell \ell })$ curve is drawn as a faint dotted line behind the markers to make it visible that the head sees the regression target only through its sampled values. \textbf  {Right:} the continuous prediction $\mu _\theta (m_{\ell \ell }) = \psi _\theta (m_{\ell \ell })^\top \bm  {w}_\theta $ returned by the closed-form attention head of Section~\ref {sec:attention} with the ridge weights $\bm  {w}_\theta $ fitted on the $K = 12$ context above; the oracle truth is drawn as a faint dashed line beneath the prediction. The asymmetry between strongly-sensitive four-fermion directions and near-SM vertex directions is the Fisher-eigenvalue split $\{19.6,\,8.7\}$ versus $\{3\times 10^{-4},\,1.3\times 10^{-4}\}$ reported in Section~\ref {sec:disclosure}.}}{7}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:oracle-distributions}{{2}{7}{Input the foundation model receives, and output it returns, for three representative Wilson configurations chosen to span the relevant behaviour: a four-fermion configuration driving an energy-growing tail (red, $c_{\ell q}^{(3)} = +0.55$ with a mild $c_{\ell q}^{(1)}$ cancellation), a moderate energetic configuration (blue, $c_{\ell q}^{(1)} = +0.30$ with sub-leading $c_{Hq}^{(1,3)}$), and a vertex-dominated configuration that barely departs from the Standard Model (grey, $c_{Hq}^{(3)} = +0.40$). \textbf {Left:} the foundation model's view at inference. For each configuration we draw $K = 12$ context points $(\mll , \mu )$ from the analytic oracle at $\mll $ values sampled uniformly on $[0.30, 2.30]$~TeV; the underlying $\mu (\mll )$ curve is drawn as a faint dotted line behind the markers to make it visible that the head sees the regression target only through its sampled values. \textbf {Right:} the continuous prediction $\mu _\theta (\mll ) = \psi _\theta (\mll )^\top \bm {w}_\theta $ returned by the closed-form attention head of Section~\ref {sec:attention} with the ridge weights $\bm {w}_\theta $ fitted on the $K = 12$ context above; the oracle truth is drawn as a faint dashed line beneath the prediction. The asymmetry between strongly-sensitive four-fermion directions and near-SM vertex directions is the Fisher-eigenvalue split $\{19.6,\,8.7\}$ versus $\{3\times 10^{-4},\,1.3\times 10^{-4}\}$ reported in Section~\ref {sec:disclosure}}{figure.caption.3}{}}
