\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces  Held-out comparison on $50$ in-distribution and $50$ out-of-distribution SMEFT scenarios drawn from the analytic Drell-Yan oracle of Section~\ref {sec:theory}. Median and fifth-percentile coefficient of determination ($R^2$) are reported, with mean-squared error on the query block. Parameter counts are matched to within $1.2\times $ across the trained architectures. The constraint-violating regressor consumes the Wilson vector on its forward pass and is not $\bm  {c}$-agnostic; we include it to set the scale of multilayer-perceptron performance with full $\bm  {c}$ information at deployment. The MSE column is the unnormalised mean-squared error in $\mu $-units and depends on the per-scenario scale of the cross-section ratio: in particular the in-distribution learned-$\bm  {\psi }_\theta $ MSE of $3142$ is dominated by a single scenario at $R^2 = 0.32$ (index $44$ of the held-out pool) where the seed context lands in a corner of the parameter space; the other $49$ scenarios in that row sit at median $R^2 = 0.9999$. The out-of-distribution pool contains no comparable outlier, which is why its MSE is two orders of magnitude lower. The $R^2$ columns are scale-invariant and are the figure of merit for cross-architecture comparison. Numbers from \texttt  {experiments/intention-vs-deepsets/output\_smeft/summary.json}.}}{24}{table.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:headline}{{5}{24}{Held-out comparison on $50$ in-distribution and $50$ out-of-distribution SMEFT scenarios drawn from the analytic Drell-Yan oracle of Section~\ref {sec:theory}. Median and fifth-percentile coefficient of determination ($R^2$) are reported, with mean-squared error on the query block. Parameter counts are matched to within $1.2\times $ across the trained architectures. The constraint-violating regressor consumes the Wilson vector on its forward pass and is not $\bm {c}$-agnostic; we include it to set the scale of multilayer-perceptron performance with full $\bm {c}$ information at deployment. The MSE column is the unnormalised mean-squared error in $\mu $-units and depends on the per-scenario scale of the cross-section ratio: in particular the in-distribution learned-$\psib _\theta $ MSE of $3142$ is dominated by a single scenario at $R^2 = 0.32$ (index $44$ of the held-out pool) where the seed context lands in a corner of the parameter space; the other $49$ scenarios in that row sit at median $R^2 = 0.9999$. The out-of-distribution pool contains no comparable outlier, which is why its MSE is two orders of magnitude lower. The $R^2$ columns are scale-invariant and are the figure of merit for cross-architecture comparison. Numbers from \texttt {experiments/intention-vs-deepsets/output\_smeft/summary.json}}{table.caption.24}{}}
